		{
			"x": -1275.8976998982066,
			"y": 222.9939324372258,
			"rotation": 0,
			"isLocked": false,
			"opacity": 1,
			"meta": {},
			"id": "shape:TLOOhPJdCppl7Iv-h9xW4",
			"type": "text",
			"props": {
				"color": "black",
				"size": "m",
				"w": 724.7502691385855,
				"font": "draw",
				"textAlign": "start",
				"autoSize": false,
				"scale": 0.8640030494240466,
				"richText": {
					"type": "doc",
					"content": [
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "All "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "bold"
										}
									],
									"text": "input and output (I/O) operations"
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": " in MongoDB, including those related to indexing, primarily run on "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "bold"
										}
									],
									"text": "RAM"
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": " for performance but are persisted to "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "bold"
										}
									],
									"text": "hard disk"
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": " for durability. MongoDB is designed to be highly efficient, and this is achieved through its "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "bold"
										}
									],
									"text": "storage engine"
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": ", which manages how data is stored and retrieved."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "heading",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto",
								"level": 2
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "How it works"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "heading",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto",
								"level": 3
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "RAM (Random Access Memory)"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "MongoDB relies heavily on the operating system's "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "bold"
										}
									],
									"text": "file system cache"
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": ", which is a part of the RAM. When a query is executed, MongoDB first checks the file system cache for the required data or index. If the data is found in RAM (a "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "bold"
										}
									],
									"text": "cache hit"
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "), the operation is extremely fast. This is why having enough RAM is crucial for good MongoDB performance, as it allows frequently accessed data and indexes to be kept in memory, minimizing the need to access the slower hard disk."
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "heading",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto",
								"level": 3
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "Hard Disk"
								}
							]
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							}
						},
						{
							"type": "paragraph",
							"attrs": {
								"dir": "auto"
							},
							"content": [
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "All data and index entries are "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "bold"
										}
									],
									"text": "permanently stored on the hard disk"
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": ". When a write operation (e.g., an insert, update, or delete) occurs, the data is first written to the file system cache in RAM and then asynchronously flushed to the hard disk to ensure data persistence. If a required piece of data or an index is not in RAM (a "
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"marks": [
										{
											"type": "bold"
										}
									],
									"text": "cache miss"
								},
								{
									"type": "text",
									"text": "), the operating system has to read it from the hard disk and load it into RAM before it can be used. This process is much slower than a cache hit."
								}
							]
						}
					]
				}
			},
